# The cases that disagree with SQLite 3.53.4 and are not fixed.
# One per line: the case's id, a tab, and why it is here.
#
# `differential_part8.rs` fails when a case here now agrees, so a fixed defect
# cannot stay listed - which is the rule from task-1969 section 4: a test that
# cannot fail is worse than none.
#
# All twenty-three of task-1979 section 6's defects are off this list: nine in
# task-1980 and the other fourteen in task-1981. Three of the four cases that
# were left - `LIMIT 1+1`, `LIMIT '2'` and `UPDATE t SET (a,b) = (3,4)` - came off
# in task-2136, when the constructs were built. The one left is not a wrong
# answer at all. It is a construct this engine has not built, so it refuses with exit code 3 - the engine's own "not yet", which is a
# documented answer rather than a wrong one, and which the suite's `Verdict`
# tells apart from a refusal for any other reason. They come off this list when
# the construct is built, and until then they are what keeps the list honest
# about what the engine does not do.
#
# One more is the other way round. `ms-007` is `DELETE ... ORDER BY ... LIMIT`,
# which the pinned build refuses because it is compiled without
# SQLITE_ENABLE_UPDATE_DELETE_LIMIT, and which this engine runs, as builds with
# that option do (task-2120). It stays here for as long as the engine accepts it.

ms-007	accepted on purpose: `DELETE ... ORDER BY ... LIMIT`, which the pinned build is compiled without
sub-010	an exit 3 gap: `(a, b) IN (SELECT ...)`, a row value against a subquery
